{
  "case_id": "2016-15-1293",
  "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Malcolm L. Stewart)",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1293",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce7908ff3073-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Because Section 52(a)'s disparagement provision places a reasonable limit on access to a government program rather than a restriction on speech, it does not violate the First Amendment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1293",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce7908ff3073-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Is copyright -- copyright a government program?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Malcolm L. Stewart)",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1293",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce7908ff3073-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Its function is to tell the public from whom did the goods or services emanate. It is not expressive in its own right."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1293",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce7908ff3073-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: But suppose the -- the application here had been for Slants Are Superior. So that's a complimentary term. Would that then be -- take it outside the disparagement bar?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1293",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce7908ff3073-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: What about scandalous?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "John C. Connell",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1293",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce7908ff3073-0001-0009",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. STEWART: They do block you from registering the mark, not -- not from filing an infringement suit or alleging unfair competition."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1293",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce7908ff3073-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Running the Federal courts is a government program. Can you say that the courts -- when it comes to trademarks, the courts are not open for actions to enforce infringement of a disparaging trademark?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1293",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce7908ff3073-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: If it's a government program, can you do anything you want with speech? Or what -- what are -- what are the restrictions that we can -- is it intermediate? You don't argue that this statute meets strict scrutiny."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1293",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce7908ff3073-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Isn't it another way to say it's not clear enough for them to get it right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "John C. Connell",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1293",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce7908ff3073-0001-0013",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CONNELL: It -- it is a -- it is a burden because our client is denied the benefits of legal protections that are necessary for him to compete in the marketplace with another band. And the only reason for the denial of those benefits is the burden on his noncommercial speech contained in the mark."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1293",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce7908ff3073-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: It seems to me -- I mean, does your argument depend upon the breadth of the government program? Let's say you had a government program putting on a -- a festival or a lecture series. We only want pro-Shakespeare presentations. It's about celebrating Shakespeare. And if you disparage Shakespeare, you can't participate. Is there anything wrong with that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1293",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce7908ff3073-0001-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: I know. But your argument earlier was that if someone slanders or libels an individual by saying -- Trump before he was a public figure -- Trump is a thief and that becomes their trademark, that even if they go to court and prove that that's a libel or a slander, that trademark would still exist and would be capable of use because otherwise canceling it would be an abridgement of the First Amendment?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Connell",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1293",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce7908ff3073-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CONNELL: I -- I think the outer limit of the protection here are the categories of historically prescribed speech. That would include threats, it would include fraud, things such as that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Connell",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1293",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce7908ff3073-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CONNELL: It does not. The register simply serves as a recordation of the marks that the government has approved according to the statutory criteria. This is in no way different than copy registration, patent registration, marriage license registration, car registrations, any other kind of typical government registrations that are simply ministerial."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1293",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce7908ff3073-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: You want us to say that trademark law is just like a public park -- the public park, a public forum, the classic example of where you can say anything you want."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Connell",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1293",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce7908ff3073-0002-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CONNELL: All of those other distinctions are viewpoint-neutral and advance the commercial objectives of the Lanham Act in terms of reducing consumer confusion."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Connell",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1293",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce7908ff3073-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CONNELL: Viewpoint discrimination is prohibited in commercial speech, no question, under the Sorrell case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Connell",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1293",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ce7908ff3073-0002-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CONNELL: Disparaging messages in trademark do not interfere with the source. They simply control the -- the other component of -- of the message."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2016-15-1293",
      "confidence": 0.0,
